> > Hi,
> >
> > I don't know the details of the Dorazio approach, as soon you're
> > thinking of a thinned point process, where you also have to estimate
> > some parameters in the 'how to thin', then INLABRU was essentially
> > written to target this question, as an extention of the original INLA
> > algorithm. (and it does other things as well, of'course)
> >
> > On Wed, 2019-06-12 at 21:58 -0700, SODE Idelphonse wrote:
> > >
> > > Hi Professor Rue and Dear INLA users,
> > >
> > > I have some questions about the Bayesian modeling based on INLA using
> > > multiple likelihoods.
> > >
> > > When reading the following two papers:
> > >
> > > - Martins TG, Simpson D, Lindgren F, Rue H (2013) Bayesian computing
> > > with INLA: New features. Computational Statistics & Data Analysis
> > > 67:68–83. doi: 10.1016/j.csda.2013.04.014
> > >
> > > - Illian JB, Martino S, Sørbye SH, et al (2013) Fitting complex
> > > ecological point process models with integrated nested Laplace
> > > approximation. Methods in Ecology and Evolution 4:305–315. doi:

> > > 10.1111/204...@10.1111/(ISSN)2041-210X.STATSTOO,

> >
> > >
> > > I found out that it is possible to fit Bayesian models with INLA
> > > using multiple likelihoods. For all the cas studies above, the sample
> > > size of the data giving the two likelihoods are equal.
> > > A typical example in forestry is the marked point process models (see
> > > Illian et al. 2013), where in addition to the observed individuals
> > > (i.e. spatial locations of a species, which is modelled by point
> > > process likelihood), we have additional information related to the
> > > health status of each species (modeled by binomial likelihood) or the
> > > diameter of each species (which can be modeled by Gaussian
> > > likelihood).
> > >
> > > However, let's suppose that, the data available for the second
> > > likelihood is aggregated in grid cells (e.g. a regular lattice), such
> > > that its sample size (say m) is less than the one of the first data
> > > (say n), of which individuals are observed at each spatial location.
> > > As example, see the Dorazio's approach proposed in Species
> > > Distribution Models [Dorazio RM (2014) Accounting for imperfect
> > > detection and survey bias in statistical analysis of presence‐only
> > > data. Global Ecology and Biogeography 23:1472–1484. doi:
> > > 10.1111/geb.12216], which includes two likelihoods: Poisson point
> > > process likelihood for the presence-only data observed at each
> > > location and Poisson likelihood for the point-counts from planned
> > > survey (i.e. aggregated data in each lattice/grid cell). The idea was
> > > to jointly model the two datases.
> > > Ne
> > > My questions are as follows:
> > >
> > > - Is it possible to apply the Dorazio approach with INLA using the
> > > multiple likelihoods framework of Martins et al. (2013)? And how to
> > > do it (any attempt of R code) ?
> > >
> > > - Can the INLA handle the sampling bias (using thinned point process
> > > models) along with imperfect detection issue as proposed in Dorazio
> > > (2014) or do we need INLABRU for that ?
> > >
> > > Any answers or clarifications will be very appreciated.
> > >
> > >
> > > Best.
