if you can look at the constraints as another parameterisation only,
then its ok.

say you have parameterisation   (a,b)

and with inla.posterior.sample() you generate N a's and N b's.

then if you want this parameterisation instead

	(a/(a+b), a+b)

then, with coherent priors, be the same as just transforming the
samples, as

	(a/(a+b), a+b)

