Hi Finn and all,

sorry to bother again - I had another thought. would it be possible to use a corner constraint instead? i.e. to estimate the effects of j = 1,2 relative to 3 for each of the covariates of the z model?

Thanks, R

On Friday, June 28, 2019 at 9:29:50 PM UTC+1, Finn Lindgren wrote:

> Hi,
>
> 1) hard to say in general, but often, yes, as long as the posterior is computable (I.e. not entirely unidentifiable, so that the numerical methods don’t collapse) prediction tends to be ok without explicit constraints. Each individual component may have a large posterior variance, but the nearly unidentifiable parts will have strong negative correlation, so that their combined effect can have a small variance.
>

> 2) not sure, but probably not one that can be specified in inla, if I understood Håvard’s comment correctly.
>
> Finn

>
> On 28 Jun 2019, at 22:23, Roberto Cerina <robertoce...@gmail.com> wrote:
>

> > Hi Finn,
> >
> > many thanks for your reply and thoughtful comments.
> >
> > I think I get your point, and I don't get the sense that this is what I'm looking for in my example.
> >
> > I have 2 follow up questions:
> >
> > 1) if I'm interested merely in prediction, can I safely ignore constraints all-together? is there any gain in predictive power from identifiability beyond being able to make interpretable inference?
> >
> > 2) is there a way in which I could use some structure of priors in order to enforce a behaviour of the coefficients which is similar to the sum-to-zero I'm looking for?
> >
> > Again thank you all for the comments,
> > R
> >
> >
> > On Friday, June 28, 2019 at 7:55:09 PM UTC+1, Finn Lindgren wrote:
> > > ...however, in some cases the constraint itself isn’t really the point, but rather to only look at wellconstrained aspects of a nearly underdetermined model. In such cases, calculating contrasts etc from the posterior samples is a sensible thing to do. It’s not clear to me if that is the case for your model setting or not.
> > >
> > > Finn
> > >
> > > On 28 Jun 2019, at 20:52, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > >
> > > > Hi,
> > > >
> > > > Yes, “adjusting” the posterior samples to impose constraints doesn’t produce the same result, unless the adjustment is done in exactly the right way (and that isn’t simple, or necessarily doable).
> > > >
> > > > A valid approach would be to throw away all samples that don’t fulfill the constraint, but that’s clearly impractical! That approach is only useful for some types of inequality constraints.
> > > >
> > > > Finn
> > > >
> > > > On 28 Jun 2019, at 20:28, Roberto Cerina <robertoce...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > > Thanks for this.
> > > > >
> > > > > I'm happy to do this 'by hand' in the posterior sample; I was worried this may have unexpected effects on the estimation procedure, and lead to different results than if one put in the constraint within the model.
> > > > >
> > > > > Is your sense I should not worry about this?
> > > > >
> > > > > Many thanks again,
> > > > > R
> > > > >
> > > > > On Friday, June 28, 2019 at 7:20:33 PM UTC+1, Helpdesk wrote:
> > > > > > hmm, we can only do constraints of the type
> > > > > >
> > > > > > \sum_j A_ij x_j = b_j,  for j=1,..., #constr
> > > > > >
> > > > > > for constants A_ij,
> > > > > >
> > > > > > ***WITHIN*** one f().
> > > > > >
> > > > > > An alternative, is to do
> > > > > >
> > > > > > inla.posterior.sample()
> > > > > >
> > > > > > and change samples to have that other constraint yours
> > > > > >
> > > > > > On Fri, 2019-06-28 at 10:56 -0700, Roberto Cerina wrote:
> > > > > > > Hi Havard,
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > thanks for the reply.
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > I understand (correct me if I'm wrong) that setting constr=TRUE would
> > > > > > > sum to zero over the effects for each choice.
> > > > > > > So for each choice j I would be constraining the effects
> > > > > > > beta_1,...,beta_D to sum to zero.
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > I'd prefer to do the opposite - namely I would like each effect to
> > > > > > > sum to zero across choices, as that is more interpretable (the effect
> > > > > > > of say age on candidate support should sum to zero across
> > > > > > > candidates).
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > Does that make sense?
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > Thanks,
> > > > > > > R
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > On Friday, June 28, 2019 at 6:01:24 PM UTC+1, Helpdesk wrote:
> > > > > > > > you can set constr=TRUE for an easy sum-to-zero-constraint. Is
> > > > > > > > this
> > > > > > > > what you want?
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > with replications, also the constraints are replicated
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > On Fri, 2019-06-28 at 09:56 -0700, Roberto Cerina wrote:
> > > > > > > > > Dear All,
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > Preface- I'm relatively new to INLA; I'm a DPhil student in
> > > > > > > > Sociology
> > > > > > > > > with a background in Statistics for Masters & Undergrad.
> > > > > > > > > My problem is as follows:
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > I have survey responses from a vote-choice question; each i =
> > > > > > > > > {1,..,N} respondent says they vote for candidate j={1,2,3}. I
> > > > > > > > have a
> > > > > > > > > very large number of covariates X (potentially p>n, but forget
> > > > > > > > that
> > > > > > > > > for now), and as such I'd like to implement a degree of
> > > > > > > > > regularisation; I'd like to do this via Ridge regression.
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > I'd like to run a poisson model (equivalent to the multinomial)
> > > > > > > > of
> > > > > > > > > the following form:
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > # likelihood
> > > > > > > > > Y_i ~ pois(mu_i)
> > > > > > > > > mu_i = exp(eta_i)
> > > > > > > > > eta_i = lambda_i + alpha_j + sum_d[ x_ijd * beta*_jd]
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > # prior on multinomial equivalency parameter
> > > > > > > > > lambda_i ~ gamma(0.01,0.01)
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > # prior on candidate intercept
> > > > > > > > > alpha_j ~ norm(0,tau_alpha)
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > # prior on random slopes
> > > > > > > > > beta_jd ~ norm(0,tau_beta_j)
> > > > > > > > > # sum to zero constraint
> > > > > > > > > beta_jd* = beta_jd - mean(beta_d)
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > In Inla the closest I've gotten to this is:
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > formula = V ~ -1 + f(id.i,  model="iid") +f(idx.j,  model="iid")
> > > > > > > > +
> > > > > > > > > f(idx, replicate = idx.j, model="z", Z = X)
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > model_inla <-inla(formula,
> > > > > > > > >                   family = "poisson",
> > > > > > > > >                   data = model_data,
> > > > > > > > >                   control.family=list(link='log'),
> > > > > > > > >                   control.predictor=list(link=1, compute=TRUE),
> > > > > > > > >                   control.compute = list(config=TRUE))
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > Where:
> > > > > > > > > V is the stacked 1/0 vote choice vector in long format, of size
> > > > > > > > N*3;
> > > > > > > > > id.i is the index for the respondent id, or rep(1:N,3);
> > > > > > > > > idx is the index of observations, or 1:(N*3);
> > > > > > > > > idx.j is the index of choices, or rep(1:3,each = N).
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > What I seem to be missing from the above is a constraint such
> > > > > > > > that
> > > > > > > > > the covariate effects by choice can sum to zero.
> > > > > > > > > I'm aware of the cross function, but I have not been able to
> > > > > > > > > implement it - if you could point me to some useful examples, or
> > > > > > > > > explain how it would work in this case, I'd be very grateful.
> > > > > > > > > Please also let me know if I've messed this up entirely.
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > Many thanks in advance,
> > > > > > > > > Roberto
